{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>בְּשַׁגַּם הוּא בָּשָׂר</b> — like בְּשֶׁגַּם with a segol. Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 6:3) explained: 'because this too is in him — that he is flesh, and even so he does not humble himself before Me; what if he were fire or something hard?' But there is no sense or substance in this explanation. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that He says: 'My spirit shall not abide in man forever' — on account of this violence, and further, on account that man is flesh and will weary in time and fail. And this is like 'גַּם — that he is flesh.' But what need is there for this argument, when it is known that they are flesh and death has been decreed upon them, 'for dust you are and to dust you shall return' (Genesis 3:19)? And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that He says: 'My spirit shall not abide in man forever,' because man too is flesh like all flesh that creeps on the earth — among birds, cattle, and beasts — and he is not worthy for the spirit of Elohim to be within him. The meaning is to say that Elohim made man upright, to be like the ministering angels through the soul He placed in him, but behold, he was drawn after the flesh, and through bodily desires he was compared to and likened to beasts; therefore the spirit of Elohim shall no longer abide within him, for he is corporeal, not divine — but He will prolong their days if they return. And this is like the meaning of 'and He remembered that they were but flesh, a wind that goes and does not return' (Psalms 78:39)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:25:41.638862713Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}